Hi Dr Broman and users of the R/qtl group,

I am using R/qtl to identify QTL on a pseudo-F1 population (This
population is derived from two heterozygous genotypes, so I coded the
genotype data as indicated in the manual for a 4-way cross). I am
quite exciting with the multiple QTL model which allows me to detect
epistatic effect, however, I encounter some problems.

This is my working chart (after data checking):
1. scantwo (method= "hk", n.perm=1000) for further stepwise penalties
calculation
2. stepwiseqtl(method="hk", penalties=pen)
3. with the result of stepwiseqtl(), makeqtk() then fitqtl() to obtain
the explained variation of each QTL.
4. lodint(rqtl) for confidence interval of identified QTL ("rqtl" is
the output of  refineqtl().

However, there was the following message when giving lodint(rqtl)

Error in lodint(rqtl) : Input must have class "scanone" or "qtl".

So I used summary(rqtl), this is the output:
    Length Class   Mode
[1,]   3    scanone list
[2,]   0    -none-  NULL
[3,] 174    -none-  numeric

So, the object does have class of "scanone".

when I took a look of "rqtl", there is only the lodprofile of 1
chromosome (for this case, there should be 9 chromosomes).

when I tape: plotLodProfile(rqtl)

the error message was:

Input qtl is not a qtl object

I've used directely the output of stepwiseqtl() for these 2 commands
which gave the same error messages.

As I was wondering it's perhaps due to the 4-way cross data, I used
the sample file "fake.4way" to run commands that I use. When I did
permutations with scantwo (method="hk") for further stepwise
penalties' calculation, R/qtl gave the following message:
Permutation 1
Error in match.arg(type) : 'arg' should be one of “f2”, “bc”

I do not know why I can't obtain the confidence interval neither why
the sample data doesn't work with me. Could you pleas help me to
understand the problem?

Thank you very much!

Sincerely,
YungFen HUANG

